Forward Proxy vs Reverse Proxy

First remember:

Forward Proxy → represents the CLIENT
Reverse Proxy → represents the SERVER

1. Forward Proxy

A forward proxy is a server placed between the client and the internet.

                Internet
                   |
              google.com
                   |
                   v
            Forward Proxy
                   |
                   v
              Client

Normally:

Client ───────────────> Google

With a forward proxy:

Client ───> Forward Proxy ───> Google
What happens?

Suppose you are inside a company.

You want to access:

https://example.com

Your computer doesn't directly connect to the website.

Instead:

Your Laptop
     |
     | Request
     v
Corporate Proxy
     |
     | Request
     v
Internet
     |
     v
example.com

The proxy sends the request on behalf of your computer.

So the website sees the proxy's connection, rather than a direct connection from your computer.

Why use a forward proxy?
1. Internet access control

A company can say:

Employee → Proxy → Internet

The proxy can block certain websites.

For example:

Employee
   |
   v
Proxy
   |
   +----> Google       ALLOW
   |
   +----> Facebook     BLOCK
2. Security

The company can inspect/control outgoing traffic.

3. Logging

The proxy can record:

User A → google.com
User B → github.com
User C → example.com
4. Caching

If many users request the same content, the proxy may cache it.

2. Reverse Proxy

A reverse proxy is a server placed between users and backend servers.

Users
  |
  v
Reverse Proxy
  |
  +----> Backend Server 1
  |
  +----> Backend Server 2
  |
  +----> Backend Server 3

For example, imagine your application has:

Application Server 1
Application Server 2
Application Server 3

Users should not directly access these servers.

Instead:

Internet
    |
    v
Reverse Proxy
    |
    +----> App Server 1
    |
    +----> App Server 2
    |
    +----> App Server 3

The user only knows about the reverse proxy.

3. Why do we need a Reverse Proxy?

There are several important reasons.

A. Load Balancing

Suppose 3 application servers exist:

              Reverse Proxy
               /     |     \
              /      |      \
             v       v       v
           App1     App2    App3

Requests can be distributed:

Request 1 → App1
Request 2 → App2
Request 3 → App3
Request 4 → App1
Request 5 → App2

This prevents one server from receiving all the traffic.

4. SSL/TLS Termination

Suppose users access:

https://myapp.com

The reverse proxy can handle HTTPS.

User
 |
 | HTTPS
 v
Reverse Proxy
 |
 | HTTP
 v
Application Server

The reverse proxy decrypts the HTTPS request and forwards it to the backend.

This is called SSL/TLS termination.

5. Hiding Backend Servers

Without reverse proxy:

User → App Server 1
User → App Server 2
User → App Server 3

Users can potentially know the backend servers.

With reverse proxy:

User
 |
 v
Reverse Proxy
 |
 +----> App1
 +----> App2
 +----> App3

The backend servers are behind the reverse proxy.

The user communicates with the reverse proxy rather than directly with the backend servers.

6. Routing

A reverse proxy can route requests based on the URL.

For example:

                  NGINX
                    |
          +---------+---------+
          |                   |
          v                   v
     /api/users           /api/orders
          |                   |
          v                   v
     User Service        Order Service

Request:

myapp.com/api/users

goes to:

User Service

Request:

myapp.com/api/orders

goes to:

Order Service

This is commonly called path-based routing.

7. NGINX as Reverse Proxy

This is a very common interview question.

You can say:

NGINX can act as a reverse proxy by receiving requests from clients and forwarding those requests to backend application servers. It can also provide load balancing, SSL termination, caching, and routing.

Example:

Client
   |
   | HTTP/HTTPS
   v
 NGINX
   |
   +----------> Java Application
   |
   +----------> Node.js Application
   |
   +----------> Python Application
8. Forward Proxy vs Reverse Proxy
Feature	Forward Proxy	Reverse Proxy
Represents	Client	Server
Located near	Client	Server
Main direction	Client → Internet	Internet → Server
Hides	Client	Backend servers
Used for	Outgoing traffic	Incoming traffic
Access control	Client internet access	Server/application access
Load balancing	Usually no	Yes
SSL termination	Usually no	Yes
Example	Corporate proxy	NGINX
Main purpose	Control/protect clients	Protect/manage servers




A forward proxy sits between the client and the internet and represents the client. It is mainly used for controlling, filtering, logging, and securing outgoing traffic.

A reverse proxy sits between clients and backend servers and represents the server side. It is commonly used for load balancing, SSL termination, routing, caching, and hiding backend servers. NGINX is a common example of a reverse proxy.

In Kubernetes, an Ingress Controller such as NGINX Ingress commonly acts as a reverse proxy and routes external requests to Kubernetes Services and Pods.


